<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rajasthan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rajasthan is the largest state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> by area, in the north-west of the country on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. It includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thar-desert">Thar Desert</a> west of the Aravalli Range and more fertile land to the south-east. Long divided among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> kingdoms, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> under British protection, it was formed in stages between 1948 and 1956 by merging those states, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> as its capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rajasthan.jpg" alt="Rajasthan" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thar_Khuri.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Rajasthan covers 342,239 square kilometres, about 10.4 per cent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s area, and includes most of the wide and inhospitable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thar-desert">Thar Desert</a>, also called the Great Indian Desert. The Aravalli Range runs through the state from south-west to north-east for more than 850 kilometres, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-abu">Mount Abu</a>, the state's only hill station, at its south-western end. About three fifths of the state lies north-west of the range. It borders the Pakistani provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and five Indian states. The name means Land of Kings, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> rājā and sthāna. It first appears in print in the 1829 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-and-antiquities-of-rajasthan">Annals and Antiquities of Rajasthan</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana">Rajputana</a>, the earlier name for the region, appears in George Thomas's memoir of 1800.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Parts of the area belonged to the Vedic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley civilisations</a>. Kalibangan, in Hanumangarh district, was a major provincial capital of the Indus Valley Civilisation, and a settlement at Balathal, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a> district, dates from 3000 to 1500 BCE. Stone Age tools between 5,000 and 200,000 years old have been found in Bundi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhilwara">Bhilwara</a> districts. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a>, according to Topsfield, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from the north-west in the first millennium CE and set up kingdoms in what is now Rajasthan. The Pratiharas, with their seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>, were acknowledged by almost all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-india">North India</a> up to the 10th century.</p><p>In 1178 a confederacy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mularaja-ii">Mularaja II</a> of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> defeated the Ghurids at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kasahrada">Battle of Kasahrada</a>. Prithviraj Chauhan led a confederacy of Rajput clans that defeated Muhammad Ghori at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-tarain">First Battle of Tarain</a> in 1191, but Ghori decisively defeated him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-tarain">Second Battle of Tarain</a> in 1192, and part of Rajasthan then came under Muslim rulers, with centres at Nagaur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a>. At the beginning of the 13th century Mewar was the most powerful state of the region. The Rajputs resisted the Muslim incursions from the 13th century onwards and preserved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> culture at their courts.</p><p>The modern state was formed from the states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana-agency">Rajputana Agency</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, which merged into the Indian Union. It includes most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana">Rajputana</a>: nineteen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, three chiefships and the British district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer-merwara">Ajmer-Merwara</a>. Jaisalmer, Marwar (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a>), Bikaner, Mewar (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittor-fort">Chittorgarh</a>), Alwar and Dhundhar (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>) were among the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> states, Bharatpur and Dholpur were Jat states, and Tonk was ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pathans</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Rajasthan is a state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> with its capital and largest city at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>. Its main features include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a> ruins at Kalibangan, the Jain pilgrimage site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilwara-temples">Dilwara Temples</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-abu">Mount Abu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keoladeo-national-park">Keoladeo National Park</a> at Bharatpur, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> known for its birds.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rajasthan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Gurjara-Pratihara dynasty, often called the Imperial Pratiharas, emerged in the eighth century in western India, in the region of modern <b>Rajasthan</b>, Gujarat and Malwa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mewar, known in its last centuries as Udaipur State, was the kingdom of the Guhila dynasty in the hills and valleys of southern <b>Rajasthan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Udaipur, whose Maharana Bhupal Singh had at first hoped to lead a union of his own, joined on 18 April 1948, and Jawaharlal Nehru inaugurated the enlarged United State of <b>Rajasthan</b>, with Udaipur as capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Jain libraries, the bhandaras of Gujarat and <b>Rajasthan</b>, preserved thousands of manuscripts, many of them illustrated with miniature paintings, including non-Jain works that survive nowhere else.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jainism">Jainism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Its heartland was the alluvial plain of the Indus and its tributaries in present-day Pakistan, together with the basin of the Ghaggar-Hakra, a river now seasonal or dry, which crosses Haryana and <b>Rajasthan</b> in India and continues into the Cholistan desert of Pakistan.</blockquote><a href="/culture/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The complex was built mainly of brick and lime mortar, the main tomb faced with white marble from Makrana in <b>Rajasthan</b> and the other buildings lined with red sandstone from Fatehpur Sikri.</blockquote><a href="/work/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal · How It Was Made</a></li></ul></section>
